WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.873
leave their brightness similar, but
a real supply may sag or overload.

00:00:01.667 --> 00:00:04.580
So parallel does not mean
unlimited free bulbs.

00:00:04.957 --> 00:00:08.473
More branches can draw greater
total current, which matters

00:00:08.476 --> 00:00:09.872
for the supply and wires.

00:00:10.570 --> 00:00:11.362
Exactly.

00:00:11.698 --> 00:00:15.468
Household wiring uses parallel
branches, but our practical work

00:00:15.469 --> 00:00:19.657
stays with supervised low-voltage
circuits, never mains electricity

00:00:20.038 --> 00:00:23.558
Resistance describes how strongly
a component opposes current for

00:00:23.559 --> 00:00:24.967
a given potential difference.

00:00:25.291 --> 00:00:26.600
It is measured in ohms.

00:00:26.863 --> 00:00:30.516
Using voltage equals current
times resistance, find current

00:00:30.525 --> 00:00:32.282
through six ohms at three volts.

00:00:33.092 --> 00:00:35.873
Current equals voltage
divided by resistance.

00:00:36.437 --> 00:00:39.158
Three divided by six gives 0.5

00:00:39.167 --> 00:00:39.654
amps.

00:00:40.089 --> 00:00:43.299
At the same voltage, more
resistance means less current.

00:00:43.942 --> 00:00:44.501
Correct.

00:00:44.928 --> 00:00:48.834
For an ordinary ohmic resistor
at constant temperature, voltage

00:00:48.857 --> 00:00:50.326
and current are proportional.

00:00:50.969 --> 00:00:55.441
A filament lamp changes temperature and
resistance, so not every component keeps

00:00:55.442 --> 00:00:57.660
the same resistance as voltage changes.

00:00:58.447 --> 00:01:01.839
For wires of the same material
and temperature, greater length

00:01:01.871 --> 00:01:03.193
means greater resistance.

00:01:03.574 --> 00:01:06.157
A larger cross-sectional area means lower

00:01:06.157 --> 00:01:06.877
resistance.

00:01:07.647 --> 00:01:08.442
Exactly.

00:01:08.782 --> 00:01:12.128
Changing material or temperature
can alter the comparison.

00:01:12.502 --> 00:01:16.279
A variable resistor lets us
adjust resistance, and in a simple

00:01:16.287 --> 00:01:19.692
series circuit, it can change a
lamp's current and brightness.

00:01:20.485 --> 00:01:24.015
But I should not claim every modern
light dimmer works by wasting

00:01:24.026 --> 00:01:25.741
energy in a variable resistor.

00:01:26.512 --> 00:01:28.505
Electronic controls can work differently.

00:01:29.275 --> 00:01:29.968
Excellent.

00:01:30.423 --> 00:01:33.922
We use a model to understand a
principle without pretending every

00:01:33.922 --> 00:01:36.069
appliance contains the same circuit

00:01:36.443 --> 00:01:39.784
Rubbing suitable materials can
transfer electrons between them.

00:01:40.404 --> 00:01:44.125
What charge does an object gain
when it receives extra electrons?

00:01:44.882 --> 00:01:45.911
Negative charge.

00:01:46.344 --> 00:01:49.265
The object losing electrons
becomes positively charged.

00:01:49.564 --> 00:01:50.795
We transfer charge.

00:01:51.039 --> 00:01:53.515
We do not create extra
electrons from nothing.

00:01:54.283 --> 00:01:54.854
Correct.

00:01:55.265 --> 00:01:59.439
In this simple solid material
example, electrons move between

00:01:59.441 --> 00:02:03.464
surfaces while positive atomic
nuclei remain bound in the material.

00:02:04.008 --> 00:02:06.936
This is not a universal claim
that positive charge can

00:02:06.938 --> 00:02:08.481
never move in any substance.

00:02:09.336 --> 00:02:14.956
Like electric charges repel and unlike
charges attract, but a charged balloon

00:02:14.958 --> 00:02:19.789
can attract neutral paper too without the
paper starting with a net opposite charge.

00:02:20.522 --> 00:02:21.054
Yes.

00:02:21.322 --> 00:02:25.646
Charge redistribution or polarization
brings opposite charge relatively

00:02:25.647 --> 00:02:27.481
nearer, producing attraction.

00:02:27.965 --> 00:02:31.648
Electric charge and magnetic poles
have some similar rules, but they

00:02:31.648 --> 00:02:33.249
are different physical properties.

00:02:34.036 --> 00:02:37.103
Earthing allows charge to flow
between an object and earth,

00:02:37.344 --> 00:02:38.895
helping remove an imbalance.

00:02:39.293 --> 00:02:43.386
Depending on the initial charge,
electrons may leave or enter the object.

00:02:44.154 --> 00:02:44.985
Exactly.

00:02:45.384 --> 00:02:49.037
Lightning is a large electrical
discharge within clouds or

00:02:49.046 --> 00:02:50.585
between clouds and ground.

00:02:50.847 --> 00:02:54.618
Static sparks can ignite flammable
vapors, which is why professional

00:02:54.620 --> 00:02:57.636
bonding and earthing procedures
matter during fuel handling.

00:02:58.395 --> 00:02:59.469
Final checklist.

00:02:59.904 --> 00:03:02.647
Current is not used up,
voltage is energy per

00:03:02.651 --> 00:03:06.276
charge, ammeters go in series,
and voltmeters in parallel.

00:03:06.699 --> 00:03:10.383
Like poles repel, and not every
metal is strongly magnetic.

00:03:11.103 --> 00:03:11.695
Beautiful.

00:03:12.105 --> 00:03:16.458
Follow the arrows, identify the complete
paths, keep quantities and units

00:03:16.463 --> 00:03:18.522
separate, and explain the evidence.

00:03:19.058 --> 00:03:22.284
Our teacher can save her dramatic
tears for a circuit diagram

00:03:22.284 --> 00:03:24.257
drawn without any junction labels
